Tests fail during release <p>Fix the failing tests on <code>main</code> before retrying <code>just release</code>.</p> Not released <p><code>just release</code> names every problem it found; nothing was changed. Typical ones:</p> <ul> <li>canvodpy pins every package: add <code>canvodpy=X.Y.Z</code>.</li> <li>canvod-store makes a minor release, canvodpy only a patch: raise   canvodpy's version.</li> <li>canvod-grids requires canvod-store&lt;2,&gt;=1.0.0, which excludes   canvod-store 2.0.0: check canvod-grids against the new major version,   widen its range in its own pull request, then release both.</li> <li>canvod-store requires canvod-utils&lt;2,&gt;=1.0.0, which excludes the   current canvod-utils 0.4.0: canvod-utils is left out of the release,   but the new canvod-store needs a newer one. Add <code>canvod-utils=X.Y.Z</code>.</li> <li>Release files have uncommitted changes: commit them in their own   pull request, or discard them.</li> <li>The branch release/v1.1.0 exists already: a branch of that name is   left from earlier work (for v1.0.0, the integration branch   <code>release/v1.0.0</code>). Delete it once it is merged, locally and on   GitHub, then run <code>just release</code> again.</li> </ul> Version is not a version <p>Use <code>NAME=X.Y.Z</code> with an explicit version (no <code>v</code> prefix): <pre><code>just release canvodpy=1.1.0    # correct
just release canvodpy=v1.1.0   # wrong
</code></pre></p> Tag already exists <p>A pushed release tag is not moved: release the next patch version instead. A tag that exists only locally can be deleted with <code>git tag -d v1.1.0</code>.</p> Draft release did not appear <p>Verify the canvodpy tag matches the pattern <code>v*.*.*</code> at github.com/TUW-GEO/canvodpy/actions, and that <code>CHANGELOG.md</code> has a section <code>## v1.1.0 (...)</code>. (PyPI publishing never triggers from a tag push at all; see step 8.) If no run started, the canvodpy tag was pushed in one push of more than three tags (step 6). Create the draft with the notes the workflow would use, from the tagged <code>CHANGELOG.md</code>:</p> <pre><code>git show v1.1.0:CHANGELOG.md | awk -v tag=v1.1.0 '
  $0 ~ "^## " tag " " { found = 1; next }
  found &amp;&amp; /^## / { exit }
  found { print }
' &gt; release-notes.md
gh release create v1.1.0 --title "canvodpy v1.1.0" \
  --notes-file release-notes.md --draft --verify-tag
</code></pre> Tag is not allowed to deploy due to environment protection rules <p>The <code>pypi</code> environment accepts only tags of the form <code>v*.*.*</code>, the canvodpy tags. Run <code>publish_pypi.yml</code> on the canvodpy tag, never on a package tag such as <code>canvod-store-v1.1.0</code>: it publishes every package tagged on that commit.</p> The dependency check finds a vulnerability <p><code>just audit</code> names the package, the vulnerability and the version that fixes it. Raise the version in <code>uv.lock</code> (<code>uv lock --upgrade-package NAME</code>) in a pull request, and tag the release on the new commit. If no fixed version exists and the vulnerability doesn't affect canVODpy, add <code>--ignore-vuln ID</code> to the <code>just audit</code> call of both workflows, with a comment saying why, and remove it once a fix is out.</p> A new package fails its first upload <p>A package that is not on PyPI yet needs a pending publisher on PyPI (the repository, <code>publish_pypi.yml</code> and the <code>pypi</code> environment). PyPI allows only one pending publisher per repository, workflow and environment, so two new packages need two runs: add the first package's pending publisher and run the workflow, then the second's and run it again. Files already on PyPI are skipped (<code>uv publish --check-url</code>).</p>
